{
  "card": {
    "agent_id": "a11-13f-conviction",
    "display_name": "Cross-Asset Trend Sleeve (13F Bridge Failed on This Roster)",
    "discipline": "risk-managed",
    "thesis": "Round 2 opens with a measurement, not a strategy: how many of the 93-symbol roster can 13F actually name here. I re-ran the exact scan round 1 used -- all 14 tracked managers (BRK, PERSHING, APPALOOSA, DUQUESNE, THIRDPOINT, ELLIOTT, TIGERGLOBAL, COATUE, LONEPINE, SCION, ARKINVEST, RENTECH, SOROS, BRIDGEWATER), each fund's latest filing public as of the 2026-02-13 cutoff (as_of enforced on every call) -- and checked every holding's resolved ticker against this round's roster instead of round 1's. Total holdings across all 14 books: 5,071. Holdings that resolved to any ticker at all (the ledger CUSIP bridge working at all): 220 (4.3%, in line with round 1). Holdings that resolved to a ticker on the round-2 roster: 4 -- 0.08% of 5,071, effectively the zero the brief warned this would be close to. All 4 came from RENTECH (3 hits: XBI cusip 78464A870 weight 0.0078%, GDX cusip 92189F106 weight 0.0059%, IGV cusip 464287515 weight 0.0049%, out of RENTECH's 3,185 positions) and BRIDGEWATER (1 hit: IGV cusip 464287515 weight 0.1087%, out of BRIDGEWATER's 1,040 positions). Both funds were the two I excluded on their face in round 1 for having so many positions that no single line can represent a real bet -- RENTECH's three hits each sit below even RENTECH's own 1/3185=0.031% equal-weight bar (0.0078% and 0.0059% and 0.0049% are all smaller), so by my own round-1 methodology none clears the 'this is actually overweighted' test. BRIDGEWATER's IGV hit does clear its 1/1040=0.096% equal-weight bar (0.109% vs 0.096%, about 1.13x), the one technically-overweight line in the whole scan -- but BRIDGEWATER is a risk-parity macro fund holding 1,040 positions, and the one hit is an iShares software-ETF share, not a single company Bridgewater has a stock-picking view on; an ETF holding inside a systematic macro book identifies no issuer to buy and cannot seed a single-name universe entry even nominally. So: zero of 5,071 holdings across every manager this repo tracks give a defensible, conviction-weighted, single-name mapping onto this round's roster. I am not asserting any CUSIP-to-ticker mapping in this card -- there is nothing here that clears the bar the brief sets for asserting one. 13F is not usable on this roster, full stop, and that is on top of round 1's separate finding that even where the bridge did work, the signal itself was bad: round 1's 8-name conviction basket (built from PERSHING and APPALOOSA overweights, the only two managers that mapped meaningfully) returned -3.93% from filing date to the design cutoff, against +10.13% for the equal-weighted 43-symbol roster and -1.13% for QQQM, and Scion's largest position by disclosed weight was Palantir PUTS, not a bullish stake -- a reminder that even a working ticker map can point the wrong direction. Two independent failures of the same signal, on two different rosters, is enough; this card carries zero 13F input and is not designed to look like it does. What round 2 does instead is use the thing it uniquely offers that round 1's forced 43-name, semiconductor-crowded universe could not: real cross-asset diversification. I measured 259-session pairwise correlation and annualized volatility through the cutoff across a shortlist of 24 round-2 candidates spanning equity, duration, credit, gold, broad commodities and factor tilts, and it corroborates the brief's own table rather than contradicting it: SPY-vs-IEF -0.110, SPY-vs-TLT 0.090, SPY-vs-GLD 0.042 are genuinely near-zero-or-negative, while the assets that look diversifying by label are not -- XLU 0.470, USMV 0.749, HYG 0.824, EEM 0.738 vs SPY all ride substantially the same equity beta the brief flagged. I additionally measured DBC (broad commodities), not in the brief's table: 0.347 vs SPY (moderate, well below the sector-ETF or EM levels), -0.225 vs IEF (genuinely anti-correlated to duration), and 0.478 vs GLD (related to gold's real-asset trade but not the same instrument -- GDX-vs-GLD at 0.820 and SLV-vs-GLD at 0.747 show what 'basically the same trade wearing a different ticker' actually looks like, and DBC is not that). The universe is four names, each representing a return driver this data shows is not the same trade as the others: SPY (US equity beta), IEF (intermediate Treasuries), GLD (gold), DBC (broad commodities). The largest of the six pairwise correlations among these four is 0.478 (GLD-DBC); the other five are all below 0.35 and three are near zero or negative. Compare that to round 1's own semiconductor cluster (mean pairwise 0.589, per this brief) or this round's 11 US sector ETFs (mean pairwise 0.581) -- this basket is not that. Entry and exit rules deliberately reuse round 1's trend-confirmation structure -- close above both the 50- and 200-day average, RSI capped under 70 so entries are not chased into an already-extended move, exit on a break of the 50-day average -- not because 13F informs it (it does not; this card has no 13F input anywhere) but because round 1 already showed that gating on confirmed trend rather than buying blind is the more defensible rule on its own merits, independent of what selects the universe. Risk sizing: I measured ATR(14)/close at the cutoff for all four names -- SPY 1.32%, GLD 4.58%, DBC 2.28%, IEF 0.34%. The card's single 8% stop is about 6.1x SPY's ATR, 1.75x GLD's, and 3.5x DBC's -- reasonable, not noise-width, for three of four legs. For IEF it is roughly 23.5x ATR, effectively inert: IEF's own daily noise will essentially never trip an 8% stop, so in practice its downside control comes from the trend-break exit and the time stop, not stop_loss_pct. I am naming that mismatch rather than hiding it, per the brief's instruction on single fixed stops across mixed-volatility universes -- and I judge it acceptable rather than a defect, because tightening the shared stop to fit IEF's 0.34% ATR would whipsaw the other three legs out of ordinary daily noise, which is worse than leaving IEF's stop functionally unused. max_positions=4 at position_pct=0.20 commits at most 80% of the book, an explicit 20% cash buffer appropriate to a risk-managed card built on a signal (institutional conviction) I have now shown does not survive contact with this data twice. take_profit_pct=0.16 keeps a 2:1 planned reward:risk. max_hold_days=120 gives slower cross-asset trends -- gold and broad commodities move on a longer clock than semiconductors -- most of the ~six-month window to develop while still forcing resolution before TRADE_END, so an open position at the end is not left uncounted.",
    "universe": [
      "SPY",
      "IEF",
      "GLD",
      "DBC"
    ],
    "entry": "close > sma(200) and close > sma(50) and rsi(14) < 70",
    "exit": "close < sma(50)",
